;; EXPECT: ok
;; TYPES: machine
;; A binary pattern against a flattened fold (design 07 §7.5, "Left- and right-associative
;; operators"). Build-time flattening stores `Sub(Sub(a, b), c)` as the sequence [a, b, c].
;; `(Sub x y)` binds one child per variable, so without `:flatten` it matches a two-element
;; node only and never fires on a three-element one (`Psub` below). Under `:flatten` a
;; regrouped view lets `x` bind the class of the prefix [a, b] when a node spells it. Building
;; `t` does not leave a node `Sub(a, b)` (the surface builder splices it), so `ab` adds one, and
;; the rewrite fires on `t`. On `w`, no node spells the prefix [c, b] during the run, and the
;; rewrite does not fire. A pattern of the stored arity, or one
;; with a rest variable such as `(Sub ..p y)`, matches without regrouping. The rest-variable
;; case runs on a third operator, `Rsub`, so that its rewrite does not put a node in the
;; class of a `T2` term.
;;
;; Correction (2026-11-05): this fixture first pinned that the `:flatten` rule does not fire
;; on `t` either (`check-neq t (T2 (Sub (A) (B)) (C))`), because a view only opened nested
;; children and never closed a run. Regrouped views close a prefix of a left fold when its
;; class exists, so the fixture adds that class (`ab`), the check is now `check-eq`, the plain
;; rule moved to `Psub`, and `w` keeps the case without the class.
(sort E)
(function A () E) (function B () E) (function C () E)
(function Sub (E) E :assoc-left)
(function Psub (E) E :assoc-left)
(function T2 (E E) E)
(function T3 (E E E) E)
(function Rsub (E) E :assoc-left)
(function U2 (E E) E)
(let ab (Sub (A) (B)))
(let t (Sub (Sub (A) (B)) (C)))
(let w (Sub (C) (B) (A)))
(let p (Psub (Psub (A) (B)) (C)))
(rewrite (Psub x y) (T2 x y))
(rewrite (Sub x y) (T2 x y) :flatten)
(rewrite (Sub x y z) (T3 x y z))
(let u (Rsub (Rsub (A) (B)) (C)))
(rewrite (Rsub ..p y) (U2 (Rsub ..p) y))
(run 2)
(check-eq t (T2 (Sub (A) (B)) (C)))
(check-neq w (T2 (Sub (C) (B)) (A)))
(check-neq p (T2 (Psub (A) (B)) (C)))
(check-eq t (T3 (A) (B) (C)))
(check-eq u (U2 (Rsub (A) (B)) (C)))
